--TEST--
`bytes` in a condition is falsy only when empty, dropping the one-octet `"0"` a `string` reads as a number — `rule:expressions/truthy-table`'s own row, asserted through both a declared `bytes` and a `mixed` holding one
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:expressions/truthy-table`'s `bytes` row is the one PHP does not hand over, because PHP
// has no such type. Its `string` neighbour is falsy for the one-character
// `"0"` because PHP reads a string as a possible number, and ADR 0009 makes
// `bytes` the type that never converts to one — so the exception is dropped
// and emptiness is the only question a buffer answers here.
//
// Every row below is asserted twice: once through the declared type, where
// the row is picked before the program runs, and once through a `mixed`,
// where it is picked from the value's tag. The claim is that the two agree,
// which is what a table with one row per type has to mean.

class Say {
    public static function declared(string $label, bytes $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return $label . " declared: true\n";
        }
        return $label . " declared: false\n";
    }

    public static function erased(string $label, mixed $m): string {
        if ($m) {
            return $label . " mixed: true\n";
        }
        return $label . " mixed: false\n";
    }
}

bytes $empty = "" as bytes;
bytes $zeroOctet = "0" as bytes;
bytes $nul = "\x00" as bytes;
bytes $frame = "\x00\x01\x02" as bytes;

echo Say::declared("empty", $empty);
echo Say::erased("empty", $empty);

// The row's whole point: this is truthy, where `"0"` as a `string` is not.
echo Say::declared("one octet 0x30", $zeroOctet);
echo Say::erased("one octet 0x30", $zeroOctet);

// A buffer whose only octet is zero is still a buffer. Nothing here reads a
// payload for a value — only a length — so the octets never matter.
echo Say::declared("one octet 0x00", $nul);
echo Say::erased("one octet 0x00", $nul);

echo Say::declared("three octets", $frame);
echo Say::erased("three octets", $frame);

// The `string` spelling of the same octet, for the contrast the row exists
// to draw. Written out rather than described, so a change to either row
// fails here.
string $zeroText = "0";
if ($zeroText) {
    echo "string \"0\": true\n";
} else {
    echo "string \"0\": false\n";
}

// `as bool` reads the same table through the conversion operator, and `!`
// through the same helper again — a position that grew its own rule for
// `bytes` would disagree with the `if` above.
bool $castEmpty = $empty as bool;
bool $castZero = $zeroOctet as bool;
if ($castEmpty) {
    echo "as bool empty: true\n";
} else {
    echo "as bool empty: false\n";
}
if ($castZero) {
    echo "as bool one octet 0x30: true\n";
} else {
    echo "as bool one octet 0x30: false\n";
}
if (!$empty) {
    echo "not empty buffer: true\n";
}
--EXPECT--
empty declared: false
empty mixed: false
one octet 0x30 declared: true
one octet 0x30 mixed: true
one octet 0x00 declared: true
one octet 0x00 mixed: true
three octets declared: true
three octets mixed: true
string "0": false
as bool empty: false
as bool one octet 0x30: true
not empty buffer: true
